  "worked_solution": "According to Newton\u2019s second law\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[(2t-9){e}^{-2t}] \\\\  & =(2t-9)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-2t})+{e}^{-2t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-9) \\\\  & =(2t-9)(-2{e}^{-2t})+2{e}^{-2t} \\\\  & =(-4t+18){e}^{-2t}+2{e}^{-2t} \\\\  & =(-4t+20){e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4t+20){e}^{-2t} & =0 \\\\ -4t+20 & =0 \\\\ -4t & =-20 \\\\ t & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude at ${t}_{\u2217}=5\\,\\text{s}.$",
